From 04854437df76fa922de5a4bf82010e1077061e85 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gennro <gennro2004@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:11:08 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: qcom_battmgr: don't wake the system on
 charger updates

The ADSP charger firmware sends BATTMGR_NOTIFICATION on every battery or
USB change, many per minute while charging. Each one calls
power_supply_changed(), whose pm_stay_awake() is a wakeup event: with the
IPCC mailbox IRQ left unmasked in suspend (as it is upstream), s2idle
resumes in full within minutes.

Register the power supplies without a wakeup source, so the notifications
are handled in suspend without waking the system. Masking the IPCC IRQ
across suspend instead (the old 20-sm8550/0502) keeps the AP asleep but
leaves the firmware's messages unanswered: about five minutes into s2idle
the charger stops drawing power, and a device asleep on its charger runs
the battery flat.

Link: https://github.com/kettlelinux/kettlelinux/commit/6f00c5e5b457b95d69137b0c086de0785c6e7b52

Rebase the insertion before the charge and OTG work initialization.
Keep the notification decoding, fixed PD requests, PPS control and PM
notifier unchanged; only the power-supply wakeup sources are disabled.

Assisted-by: LLM
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Origin: upstream commit 04854437df76 in the stable tree, after v7.2.10.
The lines are the original; what follows them differs in 7.2.10
(mutex_init() instead of the charge and OTG work).
---
 drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
index b14dc01..3befe2b 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
@@ -1723,6 +1723,15 @@ static int qcom_battmgr_probe(struct auxiliary_device *adev,
 	psy_cfg_supply.supplied_to = qcom_battmgr_battery;
 	psy_cfg_supply.num_supplicants = 1;
 
+	/*
+	 * The charger firmware sends a notification on every change, and
+	 * power_supply_changed() reports each one as a wakeup event, which
+	 * aborts suspend-to-idle within minutes. Those updates aren't a reason
+	 * to wake the system.
+	 */
+	psy_cfg.no_wakeup_source = true;
+	psy_cfg_supply.no_wakeup_source = true;
+
 	mutex_init(&battmgr->lock);
 	init_completion(&battmgr->ack);
 
-- 
